WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.760
I'm flying a few thousand feet over Lincolnshire

00:00:02.770 --> 00:00:05.614
with the Red Arrows,
the Royal Air Force aerobatic team.

00:00:05.614 --> 00:00:07.740
And maybe this is patriotism talking,

00:00:07.740 --> 00:00:11.490
but they are the best aerobatic
team anywhere in the world.

00:00:11.490 --> 00:00:13.620
Aircraft flying at
hundreds of miles an hour

00:00:13.620 --> 00:00:15.750
just metres apart from each other.

00:00:15.750 --> 00:00:17.390
Not just in level flight either.

00:00:17.390 --> 00:00:19.420
They do things like this.

00:00:24.760 --> 00:00:27.260
- Here at the Red Arrows,
we fly the BAE Systems Hawk T-Mk1

00:00:27.260 --> 00:00:29.220
powered by the
Rolls Royce Adour Engine.

00:00:29.230 --> 00:00:31.470
We've flown them here in the
Red Arrows since 1980

00:00:31.470 --> 00:00:33.140
which is obviously a reasonable time,

00:00:33.140 --> 00:00:34.810
but they are perfect for what they do.

00:00:34.810 --> 00:00:36.800
They are sprightly, they're fast,

00:00:36.800 --> 00:00:39.450
they're simple to operate, and
they're a pilot's aircraft.

00:00:39.450 --> 00:00:43.100
Today, we're going to have seven aircraft
of our total of nine in the display.

00:00:43.100 --> 00:00:47.850
We fly 6 to 11 feet apart in the air.
We're doing actually about 400 mph.

00:00:47.850 --> 00:00:51.320
The skill of formation flying is essentially
triangulating a position.

00:00:51.320 --> 00:00:53.270
So you have two references
that you're looking at.

00:00:53.270 --> 00:00:55.810
One at the front and one at
the back of another aircraft.

00:00:55.810 --> 00:00:58.300
And as long as you line up both
those references perfectly,

00:00:58.300 --> 00:01:00.450
you will be in the exact
formation position.

00:01:00.450 --> 00:01:02.710
We train for approximately seven months

00:01:02.710 --> 00:01:04.970
before we even display
anywhere near the public.

00:01:04.970 --> 00:01:07.980
We fly the same display for
almost four months of those seven,

00:01:07.980 --> 00:01:11.340
and it's a building-block approach
to get us to the stage where we are absolutely

00:01:11.340 --> 00:01:15.627
safe, professional and excellent enough
to display in front of the public.

00:01:15.627 --> 00:01:17.400
- So the question is: how is it possible

00:01:17.400 --> 00:01:21.570
to safely fly this close and
perform manoeuvres this precise?

00:01:21.570 --> 00:01:23.810
Part of it is training, trust, and talent,

00:01:23.810 --> 00:01:27.450
but part of it is a skill that
you probably already know,

00:01:27.450 --> 00:01:29.350
keeping your distance
from the car in front

00:01:29.350 --> 00:01:30.990
when you're driving on a highway.

00:01:30.990 --> 00:01:33.306
It's just that instead of
working in one dimension,

00:01:33.306 --> 00:01:36.040
these pilots are working in three.

00:01:36.040 --> 00:01:39.690
Or, if you count time, maybe even four.

00:01:39.690 --> 00:01:42.090
- My job as a team leader is to annunciate

00:01:42.090 --> 00:01:44.290
to all the team pilots where I'm going

00:01:44.290 --> 00:01:45.640
and when I'm going to do it.

00:01:45.640 --> 00:01:49.376
So we have a very metronomic
radio voice to let them know--

00:01:49.380 --> 00:01:51.660
"Coming, right, now."

00:01:52.800 --> 00:01:56.200
"Hold-ing-the-bank, now."

00:01:56.210 --> 00:01:58.270
Hold-ing-the-bank, now.

00:01:58.270 --> 00:01:59.730
Everything is done on that cadence

00:01:59.730 --> 00:02:01.660
and the guys know when I'm
going to make that input.

00:02:01.660 --> 00:02:05.090
The actual art of formation flying, in itself,
is reasonably straight forward.

00:02:05.090 --> 00:02:08.620
So the relative motion between the aircraft
is meant to be zero.

00:02:08.620 --> 00:02:11.680
And the only time that we have any
relative movement between the aircraft

00:02:11.680 --> 00:02:13.920
is when we're asking the pilots
to change shape.

00:02:13.920 --> 00:02:16.930
For the pilots who are up there, whilst they
might be upside down at 100 feet,

00:02:16.930 --> 00:02:19.390
to them the other aircraft don't
look like they're moving at all.

00:02:19.390 --> 00:02:23.340
So you can be too high or too low,
which we call too shallow or deep.

00:02:23.340 --> 00:02:25.920
You can be too far forwards or
too far backwards,

00:02:25.920 --> 00:02:27.400
which we call short or long.

00:02:27.400 --> 00:02:29.670
And of course, this being
a three-dimensional game,

00:02:29.670 --> 00:02:32.510
you can be too close,
or too tight as we call it,

00:02:32.510 --> 00:02:35.540
or too far away from the other aircraft,
which is what we call wide.

00:02:35.540 --> 00:02:36.610
But it's also about timing.

00:02:36.610 --> 00:02:39.260
So we're trying to, as the Red Arrows,
make it look like we're one wing

00:02:39.260 --> 00:02:42.540
that's moving beautifully
in graceful formation.

00:02:42.540 --> 00:02:45.280
And to do that, the guys who are
furthest away from me as the leader

00:02:45.280 --> 00:02:47.550
need to know when I'm going to turn.

00:02:47.550 --> 00:02:50.220
I expect them to have already
started putting their inputs

00:02:50.220 --> 00:02:51.820
if they're along way away from me,

00:02:51.820 --> 00:02:55.760
and if they don't, they're behind the time
and it's going to look like a broken chair.

00:02:55.760 --> 00:02:58.270
Every formation shape and
every formation position

00:02:58.270 --> 00:03:02.300
has a different route to get out of that formation
quickly and safely enough.

00:03:02.300 --> 00:03:05.620
They're what we call the escape strategies,
but every manoeuvre is different.

00:03:05.620 --> 00:03:08.400
So the pilots are not only
remembering which manoeuvre's next

00:03:08.400 --> 00:03:11.210
but also how to escape from that manoeuvre
when it's coming up.

00:03:11.210 --> 00:03:14.450
The pilots here on my team have
an inordinate amount of trust in each other.

00:03:14.450 --> 00:03:18.700
They have a prescribed piece of airspace
next to the aircraft that they're formating on.

00:03:18.700 --> 00:03:20.240
That's what we call the box.

00:03:20.240 --> 00:03:22.780
And the pilot who's flying next to them trusts

00:03:22.780 --> 00:03:26.540
that they're never going to be outside
of that box of formation.

00:03:26.540 --> 00:03:29.100
- Thank you to the Red Arrows,
to the RAF, and Rolls Royce.

00:03:29.110 --> 00:03:31.888
At some point I'll be over
in their Starrship channel,

00:03:31.888 --> 00:03:33.772
so have a look at the links on screen
or in the description!

00:03:33.780 --> 00:03:35.780
[laughter]

00:03:39.700 --> 00:03:43.420
Oh wow! [laughter]

